2026-11-07T05:53:34.6668871Z     {
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6668984Z       "id": "API-001",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6669117Z       "document": "CONTRACT.md",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6669231Z       "status": "unproven",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6670083Z       "unmetReason": "OAuth applications and the missing database, record, comment, search, member, group, file and URL-resolution APIs remain unimplemented; RL-101 settles personal-token custody without narrowing complete API coverage.",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6670204Z       "supportingTests": [],
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6677738Z       "requirement": "#### API-001 — Public REST API\n\n*Tier:* Core\n\n**Requirement.** A versioned public REST API covers pages, blocks, databases, records, comments, search, members, groups and files, described by a published OpenAPI document. It uses cursor pagination, operation keys (`SAVE-005`), declared rate limits and a stable error shape. Authentication is a personal access token or an OAuth 2.0 app with scopes (read or write, per container). Every call acts as exactly one member and passes the same checks as the UI (`SHARE-002`, `LIFE-002`, `SEARCH-005`). Tasks (`BLOCK-015`) are read and changed through it, with their assignee, due date and completion, under the same checks and notifications as the UI: assigning a member who cannot read the page is refused until it is shared, and completion records who and when. Personal-token custody follows RL-101: full members and owners only, no guest tokens, explicit container scopes bounded by current UI rights, once-issued secrets stored as hashes, a disclosed 90-day default and one-year maximum, immediate revocation, no hashes in archives and permanently inactive restored credentials. Undeclared privileged operations remain denied. Protocol and representation details are in [wiki doors](docs/wiki-doors.md).\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. a contract suite generated from the OpenAPI document runs every operation over HTTP with a token for each of two members, asserting the schemas and that every access difference matches the UI's.\n2. Expired, revoked and under-scoped tokens are refused.\n3. The suite assigns, dates, reassigns and completes a task and finds the same refusals, notifications and completion record as the UI.\n4. Issue a once-visible full-member token with finite container scopes; after revocation, expiry or restore it executes no request, and guest issuance is refused (RL-101)."
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6677982Z     },
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6678085Z     {
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6678197Z       "id": "API-002",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6678330Z       "document": "CONTRACT.md",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6678447Z       "status": "unproven",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6679228Z       "unmetReason": "OAuth application subscribers and address-pinned resolved-address egress. Default ADOPTED: owner-entered public HTTPS 443, no redirects, ten-second exchanges; real receiver acceptance pending.",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6679348Z       "supportingTests": [],
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6682562Z       "requirement": "#### API-002 — Webhooks\n\n*Tier:* Core\n\n**Requirement.** Owners and OAuth apps subscribe to page, record, comment, status, verification and member events by container. Deliveries are HMAC-signed with a timestamp, retried with backoff for 24 hours, logged with their responses, replayable, and disabled with a notice after repeated failure. Payloads carry IDs and titles only when the subscription's acting member can read the record. Protocol and representation details are in [wiki doors](docs/wiki-doors.md).\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. a Workers journey subscribes a test endpoint, produces events, verifies the signatures, forces failures and sees the backoff and the disable notice, replays one delivery, and confirms that a record the subscriber cannot read produces no payload."
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6682679Z     },
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6682781Z     {
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6682890Z       "id": "API-003",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6683025Z       "document": "CONTRACT.md",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6683147Z       "status": "unproven",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6684917Z       "unmetReason": "No validated CI-run proof for this acceptance point: \"an MCP client conformance run lists the tools, calls each one as two members and asserts access parity with the UI, confirms that a write without a grant becomes a suggestion, and confirms that revoking the client ends its session at once.\"<br>No validated CI-run proof for this acceptance point: \"With a direct-write grant, it assigns, reassigns and completes a task and finds the same notifications and completion record as the UI.\"",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6685040Z       "supportingTests": [],
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6688781Z       "requirement": "#### API-003 — MCP server\n\n*Tier:* Core · *Cites:* BASE-AGENT-001, BASE-AGENT-002, BASE-AGENT-003, BASE-AGENT-004, BASE-AGENT-005, BASE-AGENT-007 · *Register:* R-11\n\n**Requirement.** Slate offers search, cited page fetch, database listing and queries, answers, comments, page creation and updates, and task changes through its MCP door. Supported clients include Claude, ChatGPT and Cursor.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. Two members search, fetch, query, ask and comment through MCP and observe the same wiki outcomes as in the browser.\n2. Under the held company policy for standard edits, a page change is a revision-pinned suggestion and changes nothing before the person named by policy accepts it.\n3. Under the all-autonomous company policy, an authorized page update and task assignment, reassignment and completion execute with the same notifications and completion record as the browser."
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6688957Z     },
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6689130Z     {
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6689244Z       "id": "API-004",
