2026-11-07T05:53:34.6689494Z       "status": "unproven",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6690099Z       "unmetReason": "OAuth application and MCP-client administration remain unimplemented; RL-102 keeps the complete administration target partial and authorizes no carve.",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6690222Z       "supportingTests": [],
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6695059Z       "requirement": "#### API-004 — App and token administration\n\n*Tier:* Core\n\n**Requirement.** Owners see every personal access token, OAuth app, MCP client and webhook, with its member, scopes, creation time and last use, and can revoke any of them. Members see and revoke their own. Tokens expire after a default of 90 days. Revocation takes effect on the next request, and every grant and revocation is recorded in activity. Under RL-102 this complete owner/member administration target includes OAuth applications and MCP clients; API-001 governs personal-token execution custody. Secrets appear once, native browser administration uses fresh session proof and agent administration carries access and security under company policy, and activity retains grant and revocation history. Protocol and representation details are in [wiki doors](docs/wiki-doors.md).\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. a browser journey creates a token and an OAuth app, sees both with last use after an API call, revokes each and gets 401 on the next request, and finds the activity entries.\n2. An owner sees all credential types while a member sees only their own; creating and revoking uses fresh session proof and leaves activity (RL-102)."
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6695177Z     },
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6695277Z     {
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6695382Z       "id": "API-005",
2026-11-07T05:53:34.6695517Z       "document": "CONTRACT.md",
