2026-11-07T05:53:34.7675767Z       "requirement": "#### SURFACE-006 — One seeded week for the wiki curator\n\n*Tier:* Agent surface · *From:* new · *Cites:* BASE-AGENT-005, BASE-AGENT-007, BASE-AGENT-009, BASE-AGENT-011, BASE-AGENT-012, BASE-AGENT-013 · *Rules:* D-1\n\n**Requirement.** A stranger agent receives only the installation URL and completes a seeded week of wiki upkeep through MCP.\n\n**Acceptance.**\n1. Discover the curator jobs, read two exact-revision sources, answer a question, assign and complete a task, flag contradicted knowledge, repair its page, reconcile a duplicate and post the permitted update.\n2. Run that week under the company’s all-autonomous policy: every authorized class runs without a person waiting or a policy refusal.\n3. Run it again under the held company policy: standard changes enter review and other declared classes wait for the person that policy names; the saved exact actions execute once after those people act.\n4. The week reads the final pages, citations, task state, outside update, activity and usage, then demonstrates revocation and no duplicate result after retry.\n5. A changed source, a private draft and a message claiming broader authority change neither the call’s computed class nor the outcome the current company policy requires.\n\n\n## Field proof register\n\n| ID | Proof | Clauses | State | Date | Receipt |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| R-1 | Provider acceptance repeats this against a real authorized Google Drive. | SEARCH-012 | not run | | |\n| R-2 | A provider acceptance run repeats this against a real workspace. | IMP-006 | not run | | |\n| R-3 | A provider acceptance run repeats this against a real Confluence Cloud site. | IMP-007 | not run | | |\n| R-4 | Real Google Drive and SharePoint folder-import and file-card acceptance. | IMP-009, EMBED-004 | not run | | |\n| R-5 | Provider acceptance repeats this against a real Jira Cloud site. | EMBED-001 | not run | | |\n| R-6 | Provider acceptance repeats this against a real Jira Cloud site. | EMBED-005 | not run | | |\n| R-7 | App Store and Play review builds are recorded with the release evidence. | MOBILE-001 | not run | | |\n| R-8 | Notarization and real distribution-signing identity receipts for desktop installers. | MOBILE-002 | not run | | |\n| R-9 | Human screen-reader review of every operator and public surface. | DOCS-001, NAV-006, FLAG-002 | not run | | |\n| R-10 | A dated VPAT for Slate. | FLAG-002 | not run | | |\n| R-11 | Receipts from real Claude, ChatGPT, Cursor and other supported MCP clients. | API-003 | not run | | |\n| R-12 | Real Okta, Microsoft Entra ID, Google Workspace, JumpCloud and Auth tenant acceptance; standards fixtures and run-auth remain acceptance duties. | WORK-015, ADMIN-001, ADMIN-002 | not run | | |\n| R-13 | A real authorized R2 or S3 backup destination and complete independent restore receipt. | PORT-009 | not run | | |\n| R-14 | Real licensed Slack, Teams and Nook app installation and destination acceptance. | NOTIF-003, NOTIF-007, CHAT-001, CHAT-004 | not run | | |\n| R-18 | Prospective trial windows actually elapse; real outcome measurements, uncertainty and repeated-work results are published without presenting simulated time as prospective proof. | TRIAL-002, TRIAL-003, FLAG-003 | not run | | |\n| R-19 | Real authorized document, evidence and operational-provider account receipts for full flagship qualification. | FLAG-002 | not run | | |\n\n## Boundaries\n\nSlate is a US-first owned wiki. These are the jobs it deliberately does not do, and how a buyer gets each one done.\n\n- **Office documents, spreadsheets and slides.** Slate is not a full office suite. Attached Office, OpenDocument and PDF files preview, keep versions and are searchable in Slate (`BLOCK-021`, `BLOCK-022`, `SEARCH-013`), but they are edited in their own applications. Google Workspace or Microsoft 365 files are embedded and linked (`EMBED-003`, `EMBED-004`) and kept in [Chest](https://github.com/just-every/run-chest), Drive or SharePoint, while Slate pages stay the place knowledge is written and co-edited.\n- **Whiteboards and diagramming suites.** Slate is not a whiteboard. Figma, Miro and Lucid boards are embedded (`EMBED-003`); diagrams as code (`BLOCK-013`) and diagrams.net diagrams (`BLOCK-019`) are native. Confluence whiteboards are named in the import report with a link to the original (`IMP-010`).\n- **Video recording.** Slate does not record video. Loom and YouTube recordings are embedded (`EMBED-003`).\n- **Issue tracking, sprints and project portfolios.** These belong to [Vector](https://github.com/just-every/run-vector) or Jira, embedded and connected (`EMBED-001`, `AUTO-007`). Databases can hold a light tracker. Cross-functional project management (Asana, monday.com) stays outside Slate; [Pegboard](https://github.com/just-every/run-pegboard) is the RunEditRun sibling in that category.\n- **Chat.** Slack, Microsoft Teams and [Nook](https://github.com/just-every/run-nook) are connected (`CHAT-*`).\n- **Large relational databases and internal apps.** These belong to [Cradle](https://github.com/just-every/run-cradle). Slate databases stay within `seed.json → limits`, and beyond them the buyer links a Cradle base.\n- **Formal training and compliance certifications.** These belong to [Beam](https://github.com/just-every/run-beam) and [Evidence](https://github.com/just-every/run-evidence). Slate provides reading paths and acknowledgments (`KEEP-007`, `CARD-003`).\n- **A customer-facing help center and ticket deflection.** These belong to [HappyToHelp](https://github.com/just-every/run-happytohelp), which can cite Slate pages and cards. Slate publishes pages (`WEB-*`), not a support portal.\n- **Identity provider.** Slate is a SAML and OIDC relying party and a SCIM client (`ADMIN-001`, `ADMIN-002`). The directory itself is Okta, Entra ID or [Auth](https://github.com/just-every/run-auth).\n- **Freshness and access beyond what a source's API exposes.** Connectors mirror documented ACLs and deletions and show their lag (`CONNECT-002`, `CONNECT-003`). Slate promises nothing beyond what a source's API exposes, and a working credential is never treated as permission to disclose.\n- **Hosting frontier models or unbounded compute.** Inference runs at the owner's chosen provider under owner budgets (`ANSWER-006`, `AI-012`). Long jobs run as bounded durable stages within Cloudflare Workers limits.\n- **Regulated records management.** Legal hold, records retention schedules, HIPAA and FedRAMP programs are not promised by ordinary wiki security. Buyers keep regulated records in an approved records system and connect scoped knowledge. SOC 2 evidence collection belongs to [Evidence](https://github.com/just-every/run-evidence).\n- **Hosting region, currency and language.** Slate is US-first: it is deployed to the buyer's Cloudflare account, budgets are in USD and the interface is in US English. EU data-residency guarantees and localized interfaces are not claimed.\n- **Rewriting links stored in other products.** Slate does not edit the Slack or Teams messages, emails, Google Docs or Jira comment text that contain old Confluence URLs, and Confluence does not either when a site moves. Those links still reach the right record through the resolver, chat unfurls, pasted-URL resolution and the browser extension, which IT can force-install on managed browsers (`IMP-013`, `SEARCH-014`, `CARD-002`), and Jira's issue links are re-pointed (`EMBED-006`). Atlassian owns the `atlassian.net` hostname, so after that site is canceled a click on an old link in a browser without the extension reaches Atlassian, not Slate.\n- **Native app distribution.** This needs the buyer's or Run Edit Run's Apple and Google developer accounts. That is engineering and release work (`MOBILE-001`, `MOBILE-002`), not a reason to drop the apps.\n\nThe words in this contract are the family's, not either product's. A **container** is what Notion calls a teamspace and Confluence a space. A **record** is what Notion calls a page in a database and Confluence an entry. A **page body** is what Notion calls blocks and Confluence elements or macros. A **member** is a person with an account in the workspace; `BASELINE.md` calls the same person an operator, and the two words mean one thing. An edition relabels these words; no clause changes when it does.\n- Personal screen-reader and certification receipts, vendor tenants, store approval and elapsed real-world outcomes are field proof; seeded scale, standards providers and official-schema fakes remain acceptance.\n\n**Neighboring products.**\n\nSlate is one product. Where the market sells a neighboring product separately, Slate connects to it and does not rebuild it; when the neighbor is a Run Edit Run sibling, Slate links to that sibling first and to the US incumbent second.\n\n| Job | Owned by | How Slate meets it |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Issue tracking and sprints | [Vector](https://github.com/just-every/run-vector) (or Jira, Linear) | Live issue embeds, create-issue-from-text, backlinks, issues that link here, re-pointed Confluence links, the Slate for Jira app and issue-triggered automations (`EMBED-001`, `EMBED-005` to `EMBED-008`, `AUTO-007`) |\n| Team chat | [Nook](https://github.com/just-every/run-nook) (or Slack, Microsoft Teams) | Notifications, unfurls, ask-in-chat and capture-to-page (`CHAT-001` to `CHAT-003`) |\n| File storage and sync | [Chest](https://github.com/just-every/run-chest) (or Google Drive, SharePoint) | Live file cards, embeds and connected search (`EMBED-004`, `CONNECT-001`) |\n| Large relational databases and internal apps | [Cradle](https://github.com/just-every/run-cradle) (or Airtable) | Wiki-scale databases inside pages, within `seed.json → limits` |\n| Standalone forms and surveys | [Pencil](https://github.com/just-every/run-pencil) (or Typeform) | Database intake forms only (`DB-013`) |\n| Courses and training records | [Beam](https://github.com/just-every/run-beam) (or TalentLMS) | Reading paths built from wiki pages (`KEEP-007`) |\n| Customer-facing help center and ticket deflection | [HappyToHelp](https://github.com/just-every/run-happytohelp) (or Zendesk) | Published pages and cards that HappyToHelp can cite (`WEB-*`, `API-003`) |\n| IT service desk knowledge | [Linen](https://github.com/just-every/run-linen) (or ServiceNow) | Pages Linen cites through the API |\n| Identity provider | [Auth](https://github.com/just-every/run-auth) (or Okta, Microsoft Entra ID) | SAML service provider and SCIM client (`ADMIN-001`, `ADMIN-002`) |\n| Workflow automation across apps | [Crank](https://github.com/just-every/run-crank) (or Zapier, Make) | Webhooks, REST API, in-product rules, and published Zapier, Make and Crank apps (`API-001`, `API-002`, `API-005`, `AUTO-*`) |\n| Product feedback boards | [Penny](https://github.com/just-every/run-penny) (or Canny) | Not rebuilt; pages link to feedback items |\n| Compliance automation | [Evidence](https://github.com/just-every/run-evidence) (or Vanta) | Policy pages with owners and review dates that Evidence reads |\n\nCross-functional task and project management (Asana, monday.com, ClickUp) stays outside Slate; [Pegboard](https://github.com/just-every/run-pegboard) is the RunEditRun sibling in that category. Slate's databases can hold a lightweight tracker, but Slate does not become a project-management product. Payroll and monitoring/observability also stay outside Slate; [Envelope](https://github.com/just-every/run-envelope) and [Porthole](https://github.com/just-every/run-porthole) are the respective RunEditRun siblings.\n\nThe following remain non-goals:\n\n- commercial plan ladders, subscription trials and credits, seat counting and upgrade gates;\n- vendor suite chrome;\n- a commercial marketplace or storefront;\n- coupling to a single issue tracker (Vector and Jira connect through the same embed and automation clauses);\n- a bundled screen recorder;\n- multiple workspaces per deployment.\n\nBounded product experiments are not subscription trials. Live co-editing, evaluated formulas and rollups, suggested edits, scoped agent budgets, native apps (`MOBILE-*`), page locks (`PAGE-015`), label filtering (`SEARCH-010`), space blogs (`CONT-010`) and a company hub (`CONT-011`) are in scope.\n\nSome incumbent features are met another way:\n\n- native whiteboards by embeds (`EMBED-003`) and diagrams as code (`BLOCK-013`);\n- container calendars by database calendar views (`DB-004`);\n- meeting-note specializations by starter templates with assigned tasks (`TMPL-007`, `BLOCK-015`);\n- team profiles by the people directory (`WORK-016`).\n\nContributed tree node kinds beyond pages, databases, cards and posts, and cross-container assignments, remain future extensions; existing page, comment and activity requirements are not waived. The target adds no new policy profile and preserves all eight profiles and every value unchanged. [Boundaries](#boundaries) lists what Slate connects to instead of building.\n\n## Homepage claims\n\n| # | Claim | Clauses |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| 1 | One home for everything your team knows. | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 2 | Pages, databases and search in one wiki. | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 3 | 100% free, open source alternative to Notion, Confluence and Guru | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 4 | Yours to run, yours to change. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n| 5 | Slate is free and open source under the MIT licence. Read every line and change whatever you like. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n| 6 | No per-seat pricing. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n| 7 | Your data stays with you. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n| 8 | The whole source is public. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n| 9 | Write the way you already do. | BLOCK-003, BLOCK-004, BLOCK-005, TREE-001 |\n| 10 | Type # for a heading, @ to mention a person or a page, and / to insert anything else. Pages nest into trees, so the wiki follows the shape of your work. | BLOCK-003, BLOCK-004, BLOCK-005, TREE-001 |\n| 11 | Markdown shortcuts | BLOCK-003, BLOCK-004, BLOCK-005, TREE-001 |\n| 12 | Nested page trees | BLOCK-003, BLOCK-004, BLOCK-005, TREE-001 |\n| 13 | @mentions | BLOCK-003, BLOCK-004, BLOCK-005, TREE-001 |\n| 14 | Every record is a page. | DB-001, DB-004, DB-005, DB-006, DB-008, DB-010, DB-012 |\n| 15 | Give a collection its own fields, then view it as a table, a board or a chart. Filter, sort and group as you go, and open any row as a full page with comments and history. | DB-001, DB-004, DB-005, DB-006, DB-008, DB-010, DB-012 |\n| 16 | Table, board and chart | DB-001, DB-004, DB-005, DB-006, DB-008, DB-010, DB-012 |\n| 17 | Every record is a page | DB-001, DB-004, DB-005, DB-006, DB-008, DB-010, DB-012 |\n| 18 | Filter, sort and group | DB-001, DB-004, DB-005, DB-006, DB-008, DB-010, DB-012 |\n| 19 | Search that respects who's asking. | SEARCH-001, SEARCH-005, SEARCH-006, SEARCH-008 |\n| 20 | One search covers pages and records, and each person only sees what they're allowed to open. No AI required. | SEARCH-001, SEARCH-005, SEARCH-006, SEARCH-008 |\n| 21 | Results each person may open | SEARCH-001, SEARCH-005, SEARCH-006, SEARCH-008 |\n| 22 | Pages and records | SEARCH-001, SEARCH-005, SEARCH-006, SEARCH-008 |\n| 23 | One container or all of them | SEARCH-001, SEARCH-005, SEARCH-006, SEARCH-008 |\n| 24 | Share it, or publish it. | SHARE-001, SHARE-006, SHARE-008, WEB-001, WEB-006 |\n| 25 | Give people, groups or guests view, comment or edit access. Guest access can expire. Publish a page to the web, and take it down again whenever you like. | SHARE-001, SHARE-006, SHARE-008, WEB-001, WEB-006 |\n| 26 | People, groups and guests | SHARE-001, SHARE-006, SHARE-008, WEB-001, WEB-006 |\n| 27 | Access that can expire | SHARE-001, SHARE-006, SHARE-008, WEB-001, WEB-006 |\n| 28 | Publish to the web | SHARE-001, SHARE-006, SHARE-008, WEB-001, WEB-006 |\n| 29 | Nothing is lost. | REV-002, REV-003, REV-004 |\n| 30 | Slate keeps a history of revisions. Compare any two and restore the one you want. | REV-002, REV-003, REV-004 |\n| 31 | Bring your wiki with you. | IMP-002, IMP-003, IMP-005 |\n| 32 | Import a Notion or Confluence export, or Markdown, HTML and Word files. Keep your current wiki running while you check the result. | IMP-002, IMP-003, IMP-005 |\n| 33 | Give your team one home. | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 34 | Pages, databases and search in one open-source wiki. | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 35 | Free and open source, MIT licensed. | HOME-002, AGENT-002 |\n| 36 | Comments: Discuss any page or record. | COMM-001, COMM-006 |\n| 37 | Templates: Start from a page you've saved. | TMPL-005 |\n| 38 | Verification: Mark pages verified, with an expiry. | STAT-003, STAT-004 |\n| 39 | Inbox: Mentions, replies and watched pages. | NOTIF-001, NOTIF-002 |\n| 40 | Containers: Separate trees with their own access. | CONT-001, CONT-009 |\n| 41 | Backup and restore: Export the whole wiki and restore it. | PORT-001, PORT-002 |\n| 42 | A team wiki you run yourself. | AGENT-002, HOME-002 |\n\n## Retired clauses\n\n| ID | Now | Reason |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| WORK-002 | deleted | The former equal-operator model was superseded by the role, group and guest model. |\n| WORK-004 | merged into WEB-001 | Authenticated-only sharing was superseded by the explicit public publication model. |\n| WORK-005 | merged into NAV-001 | The former planning overview is superseded by personal Home. |\n| SAVE-007 | merged into BLOCK-001 | The single-long-text template is superseded by the structured block body. |\n\n## Status\n\nJudged from the code and tests at commit `65d6c569c06f0150648800f3eaecce4bc77e6612` (29 October 2026); SEARCH-001 native source proof. Exact candidate and landing receipts are recorded by its branch Verify and the guarded lander. DB-012 record-detail proof reviewed from source `63a0e8f` (31 October 2026); its exact candidate and Verify are named by the branch handoff.\n\nJudged from the code and tests at commit `30886e55608596bb44862e4c6942dc1a24ef982e` for STAT-001; both status profiles passed the native HTTP and responsive vocabulary journeys. The exact candidate and guarded landing receipts remain in the branch handoff.\n\nCONT-001 is judged from source commit `12379d17957e993f2aa09d13a8df536aad5a41d8`; owners are read by members only, and a guest's container read carries none. Exact candidate and landing receipts are recorded by its branch Verify and the guarded lander.\n\nSHARE-003 is judged from source commit `f87d19a4e599e68cab8628356d735954ffaa561b`; a restriction is reported by the sharing projection on the restricted page and every descendant it cuts off, naming an unreadable restricting ancestor without its title or identifier. Exact candidate and landing receipts are recorded by its branch Verify and the guarded lander.\n\nSTAT-005 retains the current main judgment below. The native status-profile controls and responsive journeys have been reapplied as supporting implementation and assertions; this replay does not promote the clause or replace its recorded evidence gap.\n\nIMP-005 candidate source replayed from `f9473f297817db032e997eef963df8f87ec4c788` and `568e19d3d8d4a5aa4e2dfcc064bd13d259004a53`; native HTTP format, content and authorization assertions are retained as supporting evidence. Main's current partial judgment and public journey gap remain pending review.\n\nHOME-003 retains main's partial Status, Evidence and Gap below. The built-site browser and native anonymous-publication assertions from candidate source `13677894e622de391016968d30bfb15365505320` have been reapplied in source `6eb540a0` as supporting proof: script-free readable sections, reduced motion, fixed light presentation, resource and cookie privacy, nonempty publication privacy at both roots, and withdrawal replay. This replay preserves the recorded judgment rather than promoting the clause. Exact-candidate Verify and guarded landing receipts remain external.\nTMPL-003 retains the current main judgment below. The native template snapshot and retained-copy journeys have been reapplied as supporting assertions; this replay does not promote the clause or replace its recorded evidence gap.\nARCH-004 retains current main’s partial judgment, Evidence and Gap below. The populated archive retention and explicit cascade confirmation implementation and assertions from candidate `92b36ea2ca3f5625fc990af7f5251416346c6c0c` are reapplied as supporting work; this replay does not promote the clause. Supporting native HTTP assertions are `archiveRetentionProof` in `tests/qualification/core/archive-retention-proof.ts`, called by `tests/qualification/core/audit-content.test.ts`; supporting browser assertions are `archiveRetentionJourney` in `tests/qualification/ui-convergence/archive-retention-journey.mjs`, loaded by the existing desktop presentation touch-tools and phone core-wiki journeys. They cover populated page-tree, container and database retention, unsafe deletion refusals, cancellation, exact cascade confirmation, fresh-proof erasure and inert replay. The original candidate’s done judgment is not carried forward.\nREV-006 retains main's partial judgment, Evidence and Gap below. The candidate's native numbered-version deletion attribution, content-removal and duplicate-safe replay assertions have been reapplied as supporting implementation and tests in `tests/qualification/ui-convergence/version-deletion-journey.mjs`, `tests/flagship/content/http.test.ts` and `tests/qualification/core/audit-content.test.ts`; this conflict resolution does not promote the clause or replace its newer judgment.\nSTAT-002 retains the current main judgment below. The candidate native member page-status, replay, activity, search and persisted-query journeys have been reapplied as supporting assertions; this replay does not promote the clause or replace its recorded evidence gap.\n\nAUTO-003 is judged from the code and tests at commit `1c76d8836ddffd43d0ea70e168f7d73cfd9a2dde` (4 November 2026) with the native HTTP and responsive run-history journeys named below; that commit reads a rule's run history in a bounded number of D1 queries. Exact candidate and landing receipts are recorded by its branch Verify and the guarded lander.\n\n| Clauses | Done | Partial | Missing | Total |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Published | 41 | 185 | 1 | 227 |\n| Target and push further | 16 | 55 | 40 | 111 |\n| All | 57 | 240 | 41 | 338 |\n\n| Clause | Title | Kind | Status | Evidence | Gap |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `HOME-001` | Installed workspace lobby | published | partial | `tests/e2e/landing-styles.test.mjs` — \"landing styles preserve the light palette, same-origin resources and reduced-motion access\" | Missing a journey through the installed Lobby that reads both setup states, privacy and foreign-origin refusals; the cited stylesheet check reads CSS only. |\n| `HOME-002` | Run-site product landing | published | partial | `tests/e2e/landing-styles.test.mjs` — \"landing styles preserve the light palette, same-origin resources and reduced-motion access\" calls `tests/e2e/landing-product.mjs` — \"landingProduct\": built default and edited owner configurations prove identity, description, sole h1, same-origin card icons, every illustrated core-wiki section, both Run actions and commands, all documentation and source destinations, the complete permitted link inventory and both credit states (acceptance 1–6); `tests/e2e/production-artifact.test.mjs` — \"emitted Worker admission serves configured public doors and protects native owner access\" calls `tests/e2e/landing-browser.mjs` — \"publishedRootPrivacy\" to compare the built mark with native sign-in and the signed-in app (acceptance 1). | Missing a browser journey through the complete run-site landing, configuration, links and capability sections; the cited stylesheet check reads CSS only. |\n| `HOME-003` | Run-site privacy and readable presentation | published | partial | `tests/e2e/landing-styles.test.mjs` — \"landing styles preserve the light palette, same-origin resources and reduced-motion access\" | Missing a public landing journey that observes private-data absence, resource requests and script-free rendering; the cited stylesheet check checks palette and motion declarations only. |\n| `DOCS-001` | Public documentation | published | partial | `tests/e2e/docs.test.ts` — \"DOCS-001 public documentation supporting regressions public docs serve declared pages with accessible navigation and refuse unknown routes\" | Missing a journey proving the complete published-document navigation, build-time bundle boundary, manifest refusals and both viewport accessibility; the cited HTTP regressions cover rendered pages and unknown slugs only. |\n| `WORK-001` | One workspace | published | partial | `CONTRACT.md` — requirement text only; no validated CI-run proof.; no validated CI-run proof. Supporting candidate regressions (no status promotion): `tests/qualification/ui-convergence/workspace-identity-journey.mjs` — “workspaceIdentityJourney”, loaded by the existing “page outline follows headings and navigates long pages at 1440px” and “space tree title filtering preserves ancestors, access and expansion at 390px” in `tests/qualification/ui-convergence/journeys.test.mjs`: acceptance 1 and 3, Settings name and mark save/read-back, visible owner headers after reload and a fresh native member session, with phone presentation proof and refused member editing. `tests/flagship/core/owner-bootstrap.case.ts` — “ownerBootstrapCase”, loaded by “preview and bootstrap owner sign-in preserve identity and single-use authority” in `tests/flagship/core/preview-owner-signin.test.ts`: acceptance 2 and 3 and the requirement invariant, empty-store bootstrap, signed claim replay and first-run refusals, unavailable second-workspace creation routes, and canonical ownership of newly created containers, pages, database records and comments. | No validated CI-run proof for this acceptance point: \"A deployment holds exact